course: everyday
lesson: 7
title: Swimming basics and water confidence
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Being confident in water starts with small skills: putting your face in and blowing bubbles, floating on your back, and getting back to the edge on your own.
explain: Floating on your back with your arms and legs spread, ears in the water and tummy up, lets you rest and breathe without using much energy, so it is the first thing to do if you get tired or scared.
explain: Even good swimmers can get into trouble, so swim only where an adult is watching, and at the beach swim where lifeguards are; in Australia, New Zealand and the UK the safest area is between the red and yellow flags.
explain: A rip current is a strong stream of water flowing out from the beach; if one carries you, do not fight it: float, raise an arm and shout for help, or swim along the beach until you are out of it.
explain: If someone else is in trouble, do not jump in after them: call for help, then reach out with a stick or throw them something that floats; a boy in Sydney throws a body board to his sister and shouts to the lifeguard.
explain: A boy of nine swam out past the flags at Bondi; the rip pulled him out and the lifeguards could not reach him in time.
explain: A girl in Arizona slipped into a backyard pool when no adult was watching; her mum was inside for only a few minutes, and the girl had to be rushed to hospital after she was pulled out.
fact: Children should swim only when an adult is watching them.
fact: Floating on your back lets you rest and breathe while using little energy.
fact: In Australia, New Zealand and the UK, lifeguards mark the safest place to swim with red and yellow flags.
fact: A rip current is a strong current that flows away from the shore.
fact: If a rip current carries you, you should float, signal for help or swim parallel to the shore, not straight against it.
fact: Someone who is drowning often cannot shout or wave, so drowning can be very quiet.
fact: To help someone in the water, reach or throw something that floats instead of jumping in.
fact: You should go into unknown water feet first, because it may be shallow or hide rocks.
fact: Inflatable armbands and pool toys are not life-saving devices.
fact: Everyone in a small boat should wear a life jacket.
fact: Lakes, rivers and the sea can be very cold even on a hot day, and sudden cold water makes you gasp; a gasp with your face under water can make you breathe in water.
fact: Wet pool edges are slippery, so you should walk, not run.
fact: The emergency phone number is 000 in Australia, 911 in the USA and Canada, and 999 in the UK.
fact: Drowning is quick and silent, and it can happen in less than a minute.
fact: Drowning is one of the leading causes of death for young children, and most happen when nobody is watching.
exercise: What is the first thing to do if you get tired in deep water? | Float on your back and rest | Swim as fast as you can to shore | Dive down to the bottom to rest | Wave both arms and kick hard
exercise: How should you float on your back? | Tummy up, arms and legs spread wide | Curled into a tight ball, knees to chin | Head lifted high out of the water | Arms pressed tightly to your sides
exercise: Who should be watching when children swim? | An adult who can see them all the time | Nobody if the water is warm and shallow enough | Only an older brother on his phone | Another child of the same age
exercise: On a beach in Australia, where is the safest place to swim? | Between the red and yellow flags | Wherever there are no people to bump into | Near the rocks at the end | Far out beyond the waves
exercise: What is a rip current? | A strong current flowing away from the shore | A big wave that crashes on the sand | A warm patch of water near the beach, where the sun has heated it | A whirlpool in a bath
exercise: A rip current is carrying you out. What should you do? | Float, signal for help or swim along the shore | Swim straight back against it as hard as you can | Dive under it to the bottom | Thrash about to stay on top
exercise: Why is fighting a rip current dangerous? | You get exhausted while it still carries you | The water gets warmer | It pulls you down under the sand at the bottom of the sea | It makes the waves bigger
exercise: How does a person who is drowning often look? | Quiet, head low, struggling to breathe | Shouting loudly and waving both arms | Calmly swimming on their back | Laughing and splashing about
exercise: Your friend is struggling in the water. What should you do? | Shout for help and throw something that floats | Jump in and grab them | Swim out to them and let them hold on to your shoulders | Walk away and hope they are fine
exercise: Why should you go into unknown water feet first? | It may be shallow or hide rocks | It is much warmer to go in that way | It makes you swim faster afterwards | It keeps your hair and face dry for longer
exercise: Are inflatable armbands a safe way to stop drowning? | No, they are not life-saving devices | Yes, they are as good as a life jacket | Yes, if they are bright colours | Only in the sea
exercise: What should everyone wear in a small boat? | A life jacket | Armbands on both arms | A swimming cap | A pair of goggles
exercise: Why can jumping into a cold lake be dangerous? | Sudden cold water makes you gasp | Cold water is lighter, so you sink | Cold water makes your swimsuit heavy | Cold water stops your ears working
exercise: Why should you walk, not run, beside a pool? | The wet edges are slippery | Running heats up the pool water | Running scares the lifeguard | The pool water may splash out
exercise: Which number do you call for help in an emergency in Australia? | 000 | 911 | 999 | 111
test: A girl in Cornwall feels the sea pulling her away from the beach. She is getting tired. What is wisest? | Float on her back and raise an arm for help | Swim hard straight towards the beach without stopping | Dive down and swim underwater | Keep quiet and save her breath
test: Ethan sees his little cousin slip into a garden pond and struggle. What should he do first? | Shout for an adult and hold out a stick | Jump in head first | Run indoors to find a towel | Go and get his armbands and a rubber ring from the shed
test: A family in Florida reach a quiet beach with no lifeguard. What is the safest choice? | Go to a beach with lifeguards on duty | Swim out far where the water is calm | Let the children swim alone | Swim near the rocks for shade
test: Why can a swimmer drown even when people are nearby? | Drowning is often quiet and easy to miss | Drowning always happens far from the shore | People standing nearby can never do anything to help | Drowning only ever happens at night, in the dark
test: A boy in Colorado wants to jump from a rock into a mountain lake he has never seen. What is the best advice? | Check the depth and go in feet first | Dive head first so it is quicker | Jump in where the water looks darkest | Jump only if the water is cold
test: Which is true? | Floating on your back saves energy and lets you breathe | Floating only works in salty water | Floating makes you sink faster | You must kick hard all the time to stay up on your back in water
test: A child in a kayak says the life jacket is too hot. What would you say? | If the kayak tips over, it keeps you afloat | It does not matter, you can swim | Wear armbands instead | Just hold it on your lap
test: On a hot day, Noah jumps straight into a cold river and gasps. Why is this dangerous? | A gasp underwater can make you breathe in water | Gasping makes you swim faster | Cold water makes you float higher | It is not dangerous at all
test: Why do swimming teachers first teach children to blow bubbles and float? | They are the basic skills for staying calm and safe | They are only fun games | They make children swim much faster straight away, in a day | They stop water getting cold
test: can i go in the pool, nobody's watching rn | No, wait until an adult is watching | Yes, if you stay right in the shallow end | Yes, as long as you have armbands on | Yes, if you swim quickly near the side
test: at the beach mum, which bit do we swim | Between the red and yellow flags | Anywhere with no flags | Where the water looks calm and dark | Next to the red flag
test: my mate's struggling in the lake, jump in?? | No, throw or reach something that floats | Yes, jump in straight away | Yes, swim out and grab him | Wait and see if he shouts for help first, then decide
test: river looks deep enough, dive in? | No, go in feet first, it may be shallow | Yes, if the water looks dark | Yes, diving is safest | Yes, if others did it yesterday and were fine
test: mum's gone inside 2 mins, can we still swim | No, get out until an adult is watching | Yes, it is only two minutes | Yes, if you stay near the edge | Yes, if your friend watches you from the side
test: someone's floating face down in the pool!! | Shout for an adult and call for help at once | Wait and see if they are playing | Jump in and try to lift them out of the water all alone | Run and get your phone to film it
test: Max says drowning ppl always splash and scream. true? | No, drowning is often quiet and easy to miss | Yes, they always shout very loudly | Yes, you can hear them from far away, even over the waves | Yes, they always wave both arms
test: my nan said a boy drowned at the quarry showing off, and his mates thought he was waving for a laugh | Real lesson: drowning is quiet and quick, so never swim in quarries and only with an adult watching | Real lesson: only boys who show off can drown | Real lesson: waving always means someone is joking | It is just gossip, so quarries must be fine to swim in
test: kid on the bus swears there's sharks in the local lake, that's why ya gotta swim between the flags | Sharks in a lake is almost surely a rumour; the flags mark where lifeguards watch and the water is safest | It is true, and the flags keep sharks out | It is true, so lakes are worse than the sea | It must be true because so many people repeat it
test: Grace couldn't swim at the pool party and had to wear armbands, the girls are laughing about it | Do not join in; learning to swim takes time and laughing at her is unkind | Spread it, armbands are funny at her age | Spread it, so she practises harder | Spread it, but pretend you were not the one who told
exam: It being given that a person is carried away from the shore by a rip current, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To float, to signal for assistance, or to swim parallel to the shore | To swim directly against the current towards the shore | To dive beneath the surface until the current ceases | To remove all clothing and swim with maximum effort
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the manner in which drowning commonly occurs? | It is frequently silent and rapid, the person being unable to call out or wave | It is invariably accompanied by loud cries for help | It occurs only in deep water far from the shore | It requires a period of many minutes to occur
exam: Identify the method recommended for the assistance of a person in difficulty in the water. | To reach towards, or throw to, the person an object that floats | To enter the water immediately and swim to the person | To shout instructions without taking any other action | To summon assistance only after the person has left the water
exam: Whereas water of unknown depth is to be entered, determine the manner of entry consistent with safe practice. | Feet first, since the water may be shallow or may conceal rocks | Head first, since this permits the depth to be judged | By a running dive, since this clears any obstacles | Backwards, since this protects the face from rocks
